  "max_accuracy_trial_analysis": {
    "trial": "mamba trial 6",
    "overall_accuracy": 0.768063872255489,
    "positive_accuracy": 0.9024096385542169,
    "uniform_random_accuracy": 1.0,
    "hard_negative_accuracy": 0.2712133227597145,
    "ceiling_gap": 0.10608782435129738,
    "mechanism_CORRECTED": "This trial is NOT reject-bias -- its positive_accuracy (0.902) is HIGH (close to clean shortcut-mode's 1.0), not low. Its mechanism is: strong shortcut-mode-like behavior (high positive + uniform-random accuracy, matching the shortcut baselines) PLUS a modest hard-negative accuracy (0.271) that is notably better than pure shortcut-mode's 0.000 but far from genuine discrimination (would need to approach ~1.0 alongside high positive accuracy to demonstrate real target-computation). It is best described as 'the best shortcut-mode-plus-a-small-increment trial,' not a reject-biased trial. This is a factual correction to the initial characterization of this trial's mechanism.",
    "why_reject_bias_cannot_win_on_overall_accuracy": "Reject-bias trials sacrifice positive accuracy (49.7% of the test population) to gain on the hard-negative half (25.2% of the population) -- structurally, this trade is unfavorable unless hard-negative accuracy approaches 1.0 while positive accuracy stays high, which no trial achieves. Consistent with the data: every reject-bias-mode trial in this run has overall accuracy well below the clean-shortcut-mode trials' ~0.746-0.748 and below this run's own maximum (0.768)."
  },
